  "live_records": {
    "q-126515": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126515",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14184009/assets/320a45751b2e718c66bb721cfcd78a2d206f08a9998fbbffddb1581ee995cf34.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}x\\,dy-y\\,dx,$ where $D$ is the region shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x\\,dy-y\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-(-1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (16-4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
